                <pb facs="0579" n="471" sameAs="frus:frus1927v02/pg471"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/5058: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Mayer</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-11-19T13:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">November 19,
                                1927—1 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received November 19—1
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>1009. Department’s 294, July 25, 4 p.m. Following is substance of radio from
                    commander in chief,<anchor n="34" xml:id="d557-f1"/> dated November 16: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“After consultation with the American consul general a shipment of silver
                            to Hankow by a destroyer has been made to [<hi rendition="#italic"
                                >at?</hi>] the urgent request of the National City Bank of New York.
                            This was done to protect bank’s credit and upon assurance that the
                            silver would not be used for new commitment; also, after endeavor had
                            been made to obtain permit from local Chinese authorities. There will be
                            no resultant unfavorable reaction on the part of the Chinese
                            authorities, all circumstances considered, it is believed.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Mayer</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d557-f1" n="34" xml:id="d557-f1-content">
                <p>Admiral Mark L. Bristol, U. S. N., commander in chief of the Asiatic Fleet.</p>
            </note>
